## GitNexus — automatic code intelligence (every indexed repo)

GitNexus maintains a **knowledge graph** of the code (symbols, relationships, execution flows)
queryable through the `mcp__gitnexus__*` MCP tools. **It applies to ANY indexed repo** —
recognisable by the `.gitnexus/` folder in the root and/or by the presence of the
`mcp__gitnexus__*` tools. If the repo is not indexed this section does not apply (and before using
the tools you need `npx gitnexus analyze`).

**Already automatic via hook** (`~/.claude/hooks/gitnexus/gitnexus-hook.cjs`, installed into
`settings.json` by `gitnexus setup`): `PreToolUse` on Grep/Glob/Bash **enriches** with graph
context; `PostToolUse` on Bash **checks index freshness** (warns after `git commit`/`merge`). The
hook does **not** re-run analyze on its own → **when it warns that the index is stale, run it
yourself**: `node .gitnexus/run.cjs analyze` from the repo root (fallback `npx gitnexus analyze`).
After a reindex the symbol/edge counts change: that is normal.

**Auto-reindex on every commit (new repos):** the installer registers a git `post-commit`
(`~/.local/bin/gitnexus-autoreindex.sh`) via `init.templateDir`, so **every repo created after the
install** (`git init`/`clone`) self-implants GitNexus and reindexes itself — in the background,
detached, without blocking git — on every commit, **including the automatic commits** made
following the rules in this CLAUDE.md. First commit = full analyze, then incremental. Pre-existing
repos are not covered: reindex them by hand once with `gitnexus analyze <path>`.

**Basic reflex:** to understand/navigate/modify code, **prefer the graph over grep/read**. Grep
finds strings; GitNexus understands callers, dependencies and flows.

| When… | Use |
|-------|-----|
| "How does X work?" / exploring unknown code | `query({search_query:"concept"})` (grouped, ranked flows) instead of grep |
| You need a symbol's full context (callers, callees, flows) | `context({name:"symbol"})` |
| **Before modifying** a function/class/method | `impact({target:"symbol", direction:"upstream"})` → report blast radius and risk |
| **Before committing** | `detect_changes()` (or `{scope:"compare", base_ref:"<default-branch>"}`) to check the scope touched |
| Renaming a symbol | `rename(...)` (it understands the call graph) — **never** find-and-replace |
| Tracing a bug / "why does X fail?" | `trace(...)` |
| Security review (source→sink, taint) | `explain({target:"file/symbol"})` (needs an index built with `analyze --pdg`) |
| Control/data dependencies inside a function | `pdg_query(...)` |
| Free-form graph query | `cypher(...)` · HTTP route map | `route_map(...)` · Indexed repos | `list_repos()` |

**The MUST / NEVER rules and the MCP resources** already live in each indexed repo's `CLAUDE.md`:
`gitnexus analyze` writes a block there between the `<!-- gitnexus:start/end -->` markers with the
full rules and the `gitnexus://repo/<name>/*` resources under the right name. Do not duplicate them
here: they load by themselves when you work in that repo. In short: `impact` before editing (it
warns on HIGH/CRITICAL), `detect_changes()` before committing, `rename` never find-and-replace.

**For everything else** (the full tool list, the graph schema, workflows) use the
`gitnexus-guide`, `gitnexus-exploring`, `gitnexus-debugging`, `gitnexus-impact-analysis`,
`gitnexus-refactoring`, `gitnexus-pr-review`, `gitnexus-cli` skills.
